1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,265
You have named files final,

2
00:00:02,265 --> 00:00:02,919
final two,

3
00:00:02,919 --> 00:00:03,572
and final,

4
00:00:03,572 --> 00:00:04,422
really final.

5
00:00:04,422 --> 00:00:08,671
Today you learn the tool that makes all of that unnecessary: Git.

6
00:00:09,221 --> 00:00:11,794
Git remembers every version of your work,

7
00:00:11,794 --> 00:00:12,736
who changed it,

8
00:00:12,736 --> 00:00:13,238
and why.

9
00:00:13,238 --> 00:00:16,753
And every copy of the project carries the whole history,

10
00:00:16,753 --> 00:00:20,079
so no single laptop and no single server can lose it.

11
00:00:20,629 --> 00:00:24,471
This is the first lesson of the Git and GitHub masterclass,

12
00:00:24,471 --> 00:00:26,099
from beginner to mastery.

13
00:00:26,099 --> 00:00:27,857
We will build a repository,

14
00:00:27,857 --> 00:00:29,616
open it up and look inside,

15
00:00:29,616 --> 00:00:30,202
clone it,

16
00:00:30,202 --> 00:00:33,197
and then watch the very same commit on GitHub,

17
00:00:33,197 --> 00:00:34,304
on a real screen.

18
00:00:34,854 --> 00:00:37,990
At the end there is a lab with a checker, so you can prove it.

19
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:40,433
First,

20
00:00:40,433 --> 00:00:41,283
the big idea.

21
00:00:41,283 --> 00:00:44,685
Older version control tools kept one central server.

22
00:00:44,685 --> 00:00:47,760
Everyone asked the server for the latest files,

23
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:49,853
and the server held the history.

24
00:00:50,403 --> 00:00:51,832
If the server was down,

25
00:00:51,832 --> 00:00:53,447
nobody could save history.

26
00:00:53,447 --> 00:00:54,504
If its disk died,

27
00:00:54,504 --> 00:00:56,057
the history died with it.

28
00:00:56,607 --> 00:00:57,804
Git is distributed.

29
00:00:57,804 --> 00:01:02,216
Every developer has a full copy of the project and its entire history,

30
00:01:02,216 --> 00:01:03,540
on their own machine.

31
00:01:04,090 --> 00:01:05,605
You can commit on a plane.

32
00:01:05,605 --> 00:01:08,461
You can read last year's version with no network.

33
00:01:08,461 --> 00:01:12,133
And any one clone can rebuild everything if the server is lost.

34
00:01:12,683 --> 00:01:13,676
One more thing,

35
00:01:13,676 --> 00:01:15,729
because beginners mix these up.

36
00:01:15,729 --> 00:01:17,914
Git is the tool on your computer.

37
00:01:17,914 --> 00:01:20,894
GitHub is a website that hosts a shared copy,

38
00:01:20,894 --> 00:01:22,020
and adds reviews,

39
00:01:22,020 --> 00:01:22,483
issues,

40
00:01:22,483 --> 00:01:24,139
and automation around it.

41
00:01:24,689 --> 00:01:26,067
Many complete copies,

42
00:01:26,067 --> 00:01:30,661
and one shared copy that the team agrees to treat as the official one.

43
00:01:30,661 --> 00:01:31,711
That is a habit,

44
00:01:31,711 --> 00:01:33,286
not a rule Git enforces.

45
00:01:35,336 --> 00:01:38,813
Now look inside. Git has four places your work can live.

46
00:01:39,363 --> 00:01:41,817
The working tree: the files you see and edit.

47
00:01:42,367 --> 00:01:47,444
The staging area: the next snapshot, being prepared. You choose what goes in with git add.

48
00:01:47,994 --> 00:01:51,081
The repository: a hidden folder called dot git.

49
00:01:51,081 --> 00:01:53,905
Git commit saves the staged snapshot there,

50
00:01:53,905 --> 00:01:54,693
permanently.

51
00:01:55,243 --> 00:01:57,057
And the remote: another copy,

52
00:01:57,057 --> 00:01:58,183
such as on GitHub.

53
00:01:58,183 --> 00:01:59,998
Git push sends commits there.

54
00:01:59,998 --> 00:02:00,936
Until you push,

55
00:02:00,936 --> 00:02:02,688
nothing leaves your machine.

56
00:02:03,238 --> 00:02:06,299
Inside dot git: head says where you are now.

57
00:02:06,299 --> 00:02:07,830
Config holds settings.

58
00:02:07,830 --> 00:02:11,239
Objects is the database of every file and commit.

59
00:02:11,239 --> 00:02:14,161
Refs holds the names of branches and tags.

60
00:02:14,711 --> 00:02:17,718
Delete the dot git folder and the history is gone.

61
00:02:17,718 --> 00:02:19,522
Everything else is just files.

62
00:02:19,522 --> 00:02:20,364
Remember that.

63
00:02:22,414 --> 00:02:23,587
Let us build one.

64
00:02:23,587 --> 00:02:24,001
First,

65
00:02:24,001 --> 00:02:25,450
tell Git who you are,

66
00:02:25,450 --> 00:02:28,832
because every commit carries a name and an email.

67
00:02:28,832 --> 00:02:30,005
Type: git config,

68
00:02:30,005 --> 00:02:31,178
dash dash global,

69
00:02:31,178 --> 00:02:32,145
user dot name,

70
00:02:32,145 --> 00:02:32,697
Sam Lee.

71
00:02:33,247 --> 00:02:40,052
Now the email. Type: git config, dash dash global, user dot email, Sam at example dot com.

72
00:02:40,602 --> 00:02:43,022
Git saved both in your home folder,

73
00:02:43,022 --> 00:02:43,368
once.

74
00:02:43,368 --> 00:02:45,166
Now make a project folder.

75
00:02:45,166 --> 00:02:46,203
Type: make dir,

76
00:02:46,203 --> 00:02:46,618
notes.

77
00:02:47,168 --> 00:02:49,451
Move into it. Type: CD, notes.

78
00:02:50,001 --> 00:02:53,969
Turn this plain folder into a repository. Type: git init.

79
00:02:54,519 --> 00:02:57,811
Git says it initialised an empty repository.

80
00:02:57,811 --> 00:02:58,933
No commits yet,

81
00:02:58,933 --> 00:03:00,504
and no files changed.

82
00:03:00,504 --> 00:03:02,001
Now list everything,

83
00:03:02,001 --> 00:03:03,721
including hidden files.

84
00:03:03,721 --> 00:03:04,470
Type: L S,

85
00:03:04,470 --> 00:03:04,993
dash A.

86
00:03:05,543 --> 00:03:08,207
One hidden folder appeared: dot git.

87
00:03:08,207 --> 00:03:10,427
That folder is the repository.

88
00:03:10,427 --> 00:03:11,315
Look inside.

89
00:03:11,315 --> 00:03:12,055
Type: L S,

90
00:03:12,055 --> 00:03:12,647
dot git.

91
00:03:13,197 --> 00:03:13,557
Head,

92
00:03:13,557 --> 00:03:14,060
config,

93
00:03:14,060 --> 00:03:14,635
objects,

94
00:03:14,635 --> 00:03:14,994
refs,

95
00:03:14,994 --> 00:03:16,072
and a few more.

96
00:03:16,072 --> 00:03:17,724
We will open them soon.

97
00:03:17,724 --> 00:03:18,299
For now,

98
00:03:18,299 --> 00:03:19,952
notice how small it is.

99
00:03:19,952 --> 00:03:22,755
A repository is just a folder of files.

100
00:03:24,805 --> 00:03:25,173
Next,

101
00:03:25,173 --> 00:03:29,077
the cycle you will repeat thousands of times: change,

102
00:03:29,077 --> 00:03:29,519
stage,

103
00:03:29,519 --> 00:03:30,035
commit.

104
00:03:30,035 --> 00:03:31,508
Go into the project.

105
00:03:31,508 --> 00:03:32,171
Type: CD,

106
00:03:32,171 --> 00:03:32,613
notes.

107
00:03:33,163 --> 00:03:38,688
Create a file. Type: echo, hash ports, in quotes, then an arrow into read me dot M D.

108
00:03:39,238 --> 00:03:42,609
Ask Git what it sees. Type: git status, dash S.

109
00:03:43,159 --> 00:03:46,711
Two question marks mean untracked: the file exists,

110
00:03:46,711 --> 00:03:48,870
but Git is not watching it yet.

111
00:03:48,870 --> 00:03:49,496
Stage it.

112
00:03:49,496 --> 00:03:50,471
Type: git add,

113
00:03:50,471 --> 00:03:51,586
read me dot M D.

114
00:03:52,136 --> 00:03:52,596
Staged.

115
00:03:52,596 --> 00:03:54,305
Nothing is in history yet.

116
00:03:54,305 --> 00:03:55,029
Now commit,

117
00:03:55,029 --> 00:03:57,527
with a message that says what changed.

118
00:03:57,527 --> 00:03:58,645
Type: git commit,

119
00:03:58,645 --> 00:03:59,105
dash M,

120
00:03:59,105 --> 00:04:00,157
start the notes.

121
00:04:00,707 --> 00:04:02,317
Look at the first line.

122
00:04:02,317 --> 00:04:03,157
Root commit.

123
00:04:03,157 --> 00:04:06,796
The very first commit in a repository has no parent,

124
00:04:06,796 --> 00:04:08,896
and Git gives it a short hash.

125
00:04:08,896 --> 00:04:10,296
Now a second change.

126
00:04:10,296 --> 00:04:11,066
Type: echo,

127
00:04:11,066 --> 00:04:13,515
ports colon eighty four four three,

128
00:04:13,515 --> 00:04:14,915
into ports dot text.

129
00:04:15,465 --> 00:04:18,452
Stage it. Type: git add, ports dot text.

130
00:04:19,002 --> 00:04:22,458
Commit it. Type: git commit, dash M, add ports list.

131
00:04:23,008 --> 00:04:26,933
Now read the history. Type: git log, dash dash one line.

132
00:04:27,483 --> 00:04:28,241
Two commits,

133
00:04:28,241 --> 00:04:29,063
newest first.

134
00:04:29,063 --> 00:04:31,338
Each has a short hash and a message.

135
00:04:31,338 --> 00:04:34,182
The second remembers the first as its parent.

136
00:04:34,182 --> 00:04:35,888
That chain is your history.

137
00:04:37,938 --> 00:04:40,762
Now go deeper than most people ever do,

138
00:04:40,762 --> 00:04:43,297
and open the repository's database.

139
00:04:43,297 --> 00:04:44,745
Go into the project.

140
00:04:44,745 --> 00:04:45,397
Type: CD,

141
00:04:45,397 --> 00:04:45,831
notes.

142
00:04:46,381 --> 00:04:50,925
First, where am I? Head is a tiny file. Type: cat, dot git slash head.

143
00:04:51,475 --> 00:04:52,454
Head says: ref,

144
00:04:52,454 --> 00:04:54,281
refs slash heads slash main.

145
00:04:54,281 --> 00:04:56,304
Head points to the branch main,

146
00:04:56,304 --> 00:04:59,110
and the branch points to the latest commit.

147
00:04:59,110 --> 00:05:00,415
Look at the history.

148
00:05:00,415 --> 00:05:01,329
Type: git log,

149
00:05:01,329 --> 00:05:02,569
dash dash one line.

150
00:05:03,119 --> 00:05:06,808
The top hash begins d one two five zero one zero.

151
00:05:06,808 --> 00:05:09,518
Ask Git what kind of object that is.

152
00:05:09,518 --> 00:05:11,325
Type: git cat dash file,

153
00:05:11,325 --> 00:05:11,852
dash T,

154
00:05:11,852 --> 00:05:12,228
head.

155
00:05:12,778 --> 00:05:17,770
A commit. Now print what is inside it. Type: git cat dash file, dash P, head.

156
00:05:18,320 --> 00:05:20,617
A commit is a small text object.

157
00:05:20,617 --> 00:05:21,119
A tree,

158
00:05:21,119 --> 00:05:23,560
which is a snapshot of every file.

159
00:05:23,560 --> 00:05:24,206
A parent,

160
00:05:24,206 --> 00:05:25,713
the commit before it.

161
00:05:25,713 --> 00:05:26,431
An author,

162
00:05:26,431 --> 00:05:26,933
a date,

163
00:05:26,933 --> 00:05:28,081
and the message.

164
00:05:28,081 --> 00:05:29,015
Nothing else.

165
00:05:29,015 --> 00:05:30,307
Now open the tree.

166
00:05:30,307 --> 00:05:32,029
Type: git cat dash file,

167
00:05:32,029 --> 00:05:32,532
dash P,

168
00:05:32,532 --> 00:05:32,890
head,

169
00:05:32,890 --> 00:05:33,321
caret,

170
00:05:33,321 --> 00:05:33,680
tree.

171
00:05:34,230 --> 00:05:37,180
The tree lists each file with a blob hash.

172
00:05:37,180 --> 00:05:39,217
A blob is the file's content,

173
00:05:39,217 --> 00:05:41,957
named by a fingerprint of that content.

174
00:05:41,957 --> 00:05:42,940
Test the idea.

175
00:05:42,940 --> 00:05:44,907
Hash the same text yourself.

176
00:05:44,907 --> 00:05:45,680
Type: echo,

177
00:05:45,680 --> 00:05:48,138
ports colon eighty four four three,

178
00:05:48,138 --> 00:05:48,490
pipe,

179
00:05:48,490 --> 00:05:49,965
git hash dash object,

180
00:05:49,965 --> 00:05:51,510
dash dash standard in.

181
00:05:52,060 --> 00:05:55,651
The very same hash as the ports blob in the tree.

182
00:05:55,651 --> 00:05:59,022
An object's name is computed from its content.

183
00:05:59,022 --> 00:05:59,974
Same content,

184
00:05:59,974 --> 00:06:00,707
same name,

185
00:06:00,707 --> 00:06:02,319
anywhere in the world.

186
00:06:02,319 --> 00:06:03,931
Now count the objects.

187
00:06:03,931 --> 00:06:04,737
Type: find,

188
00:06:04,737 --> 00:06:06,350
dot git slash objects,

189
00:06:06,350 --> 00:06:07,229
dash type F,

190
00:06:07,229 --> 00:06:07,595
pipe,

191
00:06:07,595 --> 00:06:07,888
W C,

192
00:06:07,888 --> 00:06:08,401
dash L.

193
00:06:08,951 --> 00:06:10,515
Six objects: two commits,

194
00:06:10,515 --> 00:06:11,140
two trees,

195
00:06:11,140 --> 00:06:11,765
two blobs.

196
00:06:11,765 --> 00:06:14,892
Everything in Git is built from these three kinds.

197
00:06:14,892 --> 00:06:17,831
And a commit's hash includes its parent's hash.

198
00:06:17,831 --> 00:06:19,144
Change an old commit,

199
00:06:19,144 --> 00:06:21,771
and every commit after it gets a new name.

200
00:06:21,771 --> 00:06:24,147
That is why history is tamper evident,

201
00:06:24,147 --> 00:06:27,149
and why we never rewrite shared history lightly.

202
00:06:29,299 --> 00:06:31,044
Now the distributed part.

203
00:06:31,044 --> 00:06:34,604
A remote is just another repository somewhere else.

204
00:06:34,604 --> 00:06:37,814
I have one in a folder called S R V slash git.

205
00:06:37,814 --> 00:06:40,397
On a real team it would be on GitHub,

206
00:06:40,397 --> 00:06:41,723
with a web address,

207
00:06:41,723 --> 00:06:43,747
but the command is identical.

208
00:06:43,747 --> 00:06:44,864
Type: git clone,

209
00:06:44,864 --> 00:06:47,795
slash S R V slash git slash notes dot git.

210
00:06:49,345 --> 00:06:54,700
Git made a full copy: files and the entire history. Go in. Type: CD, notes.

211
00:06:55,250 --> 00:06:58,919
Check the history. Type: git log, dash dash one line.

212
00:06:59,469 --> 00:07:00,809
The same two commits,

213
00:07:00,809 --> 00:07:02,150
with the same hashes.

214
00:07:02,150 --> 00:07:04,192
This clone is a complete backup.

215
00:07:04,192 --> 00:07:05,660
Where did it come from?

216
00:07:05,660 --> 00:07:06,745
Type: git remote,

217
00:07:06,745 --> 00:07:07,192
dash V.

218
00:07:07,742 --> 00:07:11,503
Origin is the default name for the place you cloned from.

219
00:07:11,503 --> 00:07:13,681
Fetch is where you download from,

220
00:07:13,681 --> 00:07:15,529
push is where you upload to.

221
00:07:15,529 --> 00:07:16,717
Now list branches,

222
00:07:16,717 --> 00:07:18,169
including remote ones.

223
00:07:18,169 --> 00:07:19,290
Type: git branch,

224
00:07:19,290 --> 00:07:19,752
dash A.

225
00:07:20,302 --> 00:07:21,339
Your own branch,

226
00:07:21,339 --> 00:07:21,663
main,

227
00:07:21,663 --> 00:07:23,541
and a remote tracking branch,

228
00:07:23,541 --> 00:07:24,707
origin slash main.

229
00:07:24,707 --> 00:07:30,342
That second one is Git's memory of where the remote was the last time you talked to it.

230
00:07:30,342 --> 00:07:33,192
Your commits stay local until you push them.

231
00:07:33,192 --> 00:07:36,366
That separation is the heart of distributed work.

232
00:07:38,416 --> 00:07:40,523
Now the same ideas on GitHub,

233
00:07:40,523 --> 00:07:41,759
on a real screen.

234
00:07:41,759 --> 00:07:45,175
This is a real capture of a private repository,

235
00:07:45,175 --> 00:07:46,991
assembled frame by frame,

236
00:07:46,991 --> 00:07:48,663
with every click shown.

237
00:07:48,663 --> 00:07:51,570
I created an empty repository and chose,

238
00:07:51,570 --> 00:07:52,878
create a new file.

239
00:07:52,878 --> 00:07:54,113
The editor opens.

240
00:07:54,113 --> 00:07:56,293
Name the file read me dot M D.

241
00:07:56,843 --> 00:08:01,174
Add a heading and one line of text. When you are ready, click Commit changes.

242
00:08:01,724 --> 00:08:03,959
GitHub asks for the commit message.

243
00:08:03,959 --> 00:08:07,725
It is the same thing we typed after dash M in the terminal.

244
00:08:07,725 --> 00:08:08,811
Say what changed.

245
00:08:08,811 --> 00:08:10,471
Then click Commit changes.

246
00:08:11,021 --> 00:08:11,333
Done.

247
00:08:11,333 --> 00:08:13,835
GitHub made a commit on the main branch.

248
00:08:13,835 --> 00:08:15,024
See the short hash,

249
00:08:15,024 --> 00:08:16,713
e four eight five c a five,

250
00:08:16,713 --> 00:08:18,464
and the counter: one commit.

251
00:08:18,464 --> 00:08:20,215
Click it to see the history.

252
00:08:20,765 --> 00:08:25,309
The history lists the commit with its message and the same short hash. Open it.

253
00:08:25,859 --> 00:08:27,520
Here is the commit itself.

254
00:08:27,520 --> 00:08:29,564
Look on the right: zero parents.

255
00:08:29,564 --> 00:08:30,969
This is a root commit,

256
00:08:30,969 --> 00:08:33,907
exactly like our first commit in the terminal.

257
00:08:33,907 --> 00:08:36,590
The green lines are what the commit added.

258
00:08:37,140 --> 00:08:38,131
One more place.

259
00:08:38,131 --> 00:08:39,651
On the repository page,

260
00:08:39,651 --> 00:08:43,350
the Code button shows the address you give to git clone.

261
00:08:43,350 --> 00:08:43,879
Copy it,

262
00:08:43,879 --> 00:08:46,125
and you can clone this repository,

263
00:08:46,125 --> 00:08:48,041
just as we did from a folder.

264
00:08:48,591 --> 00:08:49,469
Same objects,

265
00:08:49,469 --> 00:08:50,279
same hashes,

266
00:08:50,279 --> 00:08:51,156
same history.

267
00:08:51,156 --> 00:08:55,610
The terminal and the web page are two windows onto one repository.

268
00:08:57,960 --> 00:09:03,144
Your task, in your own Ubuntu virtual machine. Everything you need is in one place.

269
00:09:03,694 --> 00:09:06,344
The repository is git hub dot com,

270
00:09:06,344 --> 00:09:07,903
slash it dash waves,

271
00:09:07,903 --> 00:09:09,696
slash dev ops dash A I.

272
00:09:09,696 --> 00:09:11,255
The lab is one file,

273
00:09:11,255 --> 00:09:13,125
lab fifty-seven dot S H,

274
00:09:13,125 --> 00:09:15,619
in the academy slash lab folder.

275
00:09:16,169 --> 00:09:17,756
Install it like before,

276
00:09:17,756 --> 00:09:19,825
and source the shortcuts file.

277
00:09:19,825 --> 00:09:20,929
Then run lab up.

278
00:09:20,929 --> 00:09:24,516
It builds a shared repository called origin dot git,

279
00:09:24,516 --> 00:09:25,620
with one commit.

280
00:09:26,170 --> 00:09:30,735
One: clone origin dot git into the work folder, as a folder called team.

281
00:09:31,285 --> 00:09:35,872
Two: inside your clone, create notes dot text, and make one commit of your own.

282
00:09:36,422 --> 00:09:38,204
